  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1246 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nAs we head into the late afternoon and evening hours a second upper \nlevel trough moving along the Gulf coast will help to interject \nbetter moisture streaming in from the south. This will help to \ninstigate precipitation along an inverted trough that will pivot \nacross the Midwest. Along this boundary an area of FGEN is expected \nto stretch from western TN up into IL. The latest 12Z guidance stops \nshort of bringing the strongest forcing into our southern counties \nbut will still be enough to produce some wintry precipitation given \nthe late arrival. Aloft we will have sufficient cold air within the \nDGZ to produce snowflakes with sounding profiles showing an absence \nof a warm nose in the low levels. One tricky part will be the \nsurface temperatures as most guidance have them floating around the \nfreezing mark. The sector of the strongest forcing will also play a \nsignificant role as higher rates could overpower some of the warm \nsurface temperatures. While the best forcing still looks to be to \nour south it is worth noting that the ECMWF shift of tails is \nsitting between a 1-2 south of the Quad Cities, indicative of some \nhigher totals possible. As it stands the current probabilities of at \nleast 1 inch of snow range from 60-80% along our southern half and \n20-40% across the northern half. Temperatures will rebound on \nThanksgiving day with snow transitioning to rain as highs climb into \nthe upper 30s and low 40s. Expect precipitation to exit to the east \nThursday night. \n\nQuieter weather arrives on Friday with ridging aloft moving through \nthe pattern. Highs will warm over the next several days eventually \nclimbing back into the 50s for Saturday. Cluster analysis shows a \nstrong agreement for a surface low developing in the TX Panhandle \nSaturday evening. The progression of this low remains uncertain \namongst ensemble guidance. For now, the NBM is carrying 20-30% PoP \nSaturday evening into Sunday with rain being the dominant \nprecipitation type.",
